## Problem

Once there is a second app, "who is this user?" stops being a question either app
should answer on its own. shoppingo and jewellery-catalogue share the same
people — the sharing features in shoppingo look users up by username — and
duplicating registration, password hashing, token signing and session storage in
both means two places to get session expiry wrong and two secrets to rotate in
lockstep. That duplication was real, not hypothetical: shoppingo carried its own
login, register and auth-context components until they were deleted in September
2025 in favour of kivo plus a shared client.

## Approach

Pull authentication out into one small service with no UI. It owns registration
and login, issues a short-lived access token plus an httpOnly refresh cookie, and
exposes `/verify` so a consuming API can turn a bearer token into a user id
without knowing anything about how it was signed. `/search` and `/users` let the
consuming apps resolve usernames for their sharing features. `/health` and
`/metrics` make it observable from the host's Gatus and Prometheus stacks.

## Architecture

Hono on Bun, TypeScript, MongoDB via the native driver. One directory per
endpoint under `src/routes/` — `login`, `register`, `refresh`, `verify`,
`logout`, `search`, `users` — with the shared plumbing (config validation, DI
container, structured logging, Mongo connection) coming from
[`@imapps/api-utils`](/projects/utils/) rather than being written again here.
That is deliberate: this repo should be almost nothing but the auth logic.

Middleware runs as a fixed stack: CORS allow-list → request logging →
Cloudflare header handling → security headers → an in-memory rate limiter that
skips `/health` and `/metrics` → Prometheus request metrics.

Sessions are their own MongoDB collection. Only the SHA-256 hash of a refresh
token is stored, and a TTL index on `createdAt` (30 days) expires stale rows
with no cron job anywhere.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**Rotate the refresh token on every use, and store only its hash.** `/refresh`
verifies the JWT, checks its `aud: "kivo"` claim, looks the hash up in
`sessions`, then signs a *new* pair, inserts the new session row and deletes the
old one. The simpler design — a long-lived refresh token checked only by
signature — is stateless and needs no database, but it also cannot be revoked
and leaks permanently if it leaks at all. Here the access token stays short and
stateless while the refresh token is the revocable thing; `/logout` deletes the
session so the cookie is dead on arrival. Storing the hash rather than the token
means a database dump is not a set of live sessions.

**A service, not a shared library.** A library would have avoided a network hop
on every authorised request, which is the real cost of this design. But a
library is copied into each app's process with each app's own copy of the
session collection and its own deploy cadence — so "revoke this session" and
"rotate the signing secret" become N-app operations. One `/verify` hop buys one
place where sessions exist.

**Rate limiting is in-memory, and that is a bounded bet.** kivo runs as a single
instance, so a Redis-backed limiter would be infrastructure bought for a scale
that does not exist. It is a middleware function with a narrow surface; if this
ever runs as more than one replica, the limiter is the thing that gets swapped,
not the thing that has to be untangled.

## Outcome

Runs on the self-hosted Dokploy host as an internal service, monitored by Gatus
against `/health` and scraped by Prometheus. shoppingo and jewellery-catalogue
both authenticate through it; both also ship a local `mock-auth-server` so their
development loop does not depend on it being up.

The obvious next step is that the in-memory rate limiter and the single instance
are the same assumption wearing two hats — the first time this needs a second
replica, both have to change together.
